      "exp_en": "This is an overflowing/surging/flooding pulse (hong mai) combined with an empty pulse (xu mai) and a rapid pulse (shu mai). The overflowing pulse (hong mai) has also been described as big and extending beyond the pulse position, superficial and feeling as if it overflows the normal pulse channel. CAM describes it being like a roaring wave which comes on strong and then fades away. If the surging pulse (hong mai) does not have the momentum of a roaring wave then it is called a large or big pulse. The overflowing pulse (hong mai) is indicative of extreme excess Heat that can occur with fevers or interior Hot conditions. If the pulse is overflowing (hong mai) and empty (xu mai) then it is indicative of Empty-Fire due to deficiency of yin. The surging pulse (hong mai) is often felt with the rapid pulse (shu mai). An empty pulse (xu mai) is soft and forceless upon slight pressure. The point combination HT-6 (yin xi), KI-3 (tai xi) clear Empty-Fire and nourish the yin. HT-6 (yin xi) clears Empty-Fire and nourishes Heart yin. KI-3 (tai xi) tonifies Kidney yin. Together these points harmonize the Heart and Kidney. Foundations of Chinese Medicine, Maciocia, 2005, pages 363, 608",
      "exp_hant": "此為洪脈兼虛脈與數脈。洪脈亦被描述為脈體寬大、超出正常脈位、浮取即得，感覺如洪水氾濫脈道。CAM將其描述為如洶湧波濤，來時勢盛，去時漸衰。若洪脈缺乏這種波濤洶湧之勢，則稱為大脈。洪脈主極盛之實熱，可見於發熱或內熱證。若脈洪而虛，則主陰虛所致之虛火。洪脈常與數脈並見。虛脈輕按則柔軟無力。陰郄配太谿此穴組能清虛火、滋陰。陰郄清虛火、養心陰。太谿補腎陰。兩穴合用可調和心腎。(出處：Foundations of Chinese Medicine，Maciocia，2005年版，第363、608頁)"
