      "exp_en": "Liver Yang Rising. LR-3 (tai chong) is the main distal point to use and is selected to subdue Liver yang. TB-5 (wai guan) subdues Liver yang especially as it affects headaches on the Gall Bladder channel. GB-43 (xia xi) subdues Liver yang and is the main distal point for head pain around the eyes and temple. GB-20 (feng chi) is a local point to subdue hyperactive Liver yang from the head. SP-6 (san yin jiao), KI-3 (tai xi), and LR-8 (qu quan) nourish Liver yin. GB-8 (shuai gu) is local point that treats headaches from excess Liver yang. Additional points to select include: LI-4 (he gu) to regulate the up and down movement of qi. GB-38 (yang fu) is selected for chronic and difficult migraines. ST-36 (zu san li) is selected to nourish the Blood. Other local points to consider in subduing Liver yang depending on the location of the headache are BL-2 (zan zhu), extra point tai yang, GB-9 (tian chong), and GB-6 (xuan li). Reducing needle technique is used on those points which calm the hyperactive yang while reinforcing needle technique is used on those points that nourish Blood and yin. The Foundations of Chinese Medicine, Maciocia, 2015, page 552; 2005 Edition, page 522; 1989 Edition page 225",
